 "about": {
  "text": "It’s not as if you wake up one morning and your neighborhood has suddenly changed. Rather, it’s a creeping process. People move away, stores close down, houses are torn down, and vacant lots disappear. Nothing good comes in its place: cheap, cookie-cutter architecture for the unimaginative, featuring apartments in a pseudo-exclusive, monotonous design at astronomical prices; the density of snobs rises exponentially with the rent. Does this sound familiar to you, too? The process is called gentrification. The word refers to a circulation pump that helps capital generate even more capital. The end result is a dead neighborhood where you’ll find cell phone stores, nail salons, and hookah bars, but nothing you really need to live—let alone anything that’s affordable.",
  "lang": "en",
  "venue": "Xenix",
  "url": "https://www.xenix.ch/programm/oktober-2026/schoener-wohnen--geschichten-der-gentrifizierung",
  "translated": true
